  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The green webcap (Thaxterogaster scaurus) is a striking fungus in the Webcaps family characterized by its distinctive green coloring and delicate webbed partial veil. It is a mycorrhizal species found in forest ecosystems throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Green coloration",
      "description": "Bright green to yellowish-green cap and stem that may fade with age",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Webbed veil",
      "description": "Distinctive cobweb-like partial veil connecting cap margin to stem, characteristic of webcaps",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill attachment",
      "description": "Rusty-brown gills that mature from pale color, typical of cortinarius species",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "The green webcap typically grows in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests in New England, forming mycorrhizal associations with tree roots on moist forest floors.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like many webcaps, the green webcap's distinctive cobweb-like veil (cortina) dissolves as the mushroom matures, leaving only delicate traces on the stem and gill edges.",
